<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">People's Secretariat</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1917">1917</a>–<a href="/years/1918">1918</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The People's Secretariat was the government of the Bolshevik-led Ukrainian People's Republic of Soviets, formed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kharkiv">Kharkiv</a> in December 1917 as a rival to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-rada">Central Rada</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kyiv</a>. Its members included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yevgenia-bosch">Yevhenia Bosh</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mykola-skrypnyk">Mykola Skrypnyk</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volodymyr-zatonsky">Volodymyr Zatonsky</a>. It depended on Red Guards from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> and was dissolved in 1918 after the German and Austro-Hungarian advance into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/peoples-secretariat.jpg" alt="People's Secretariat" width="320" height="357" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Emblem_of_the_Ukrainian_Soviet_Socialist_Republic_(1949%E2%80%931991),_Emblem_of_Ukraine_(1991%E2%80%931992).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The People's Secretariat was the executive body of the Provisional Central Executive Committee of Soviets in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a>. It was formed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kharkiv">Kharkiv</a> on 30 December 1917 by Russian and local <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolsheviks">Bolsheviks</a> as a Ukrainian Soviet government in opposition to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-rada">Central Rada</a> and its General Secretariat, and it claimed the same jurisdiction over Ukraine. The Central Executive Committee elected by the First All-Ukrainian Congress of Soviets cancelled the declaration of independence, declared Ukraine federally subordinate to the Russian SFSR and called for a fight against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/radaa">Rada</a>.</p><p>All its secretaries belonged to the Russian Social Democratic Labour Party and to the RSDRP(b)-SDU, founded at a regional congress in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kiev</a> on 3 December 1917 and led by a Chief Committee under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yevgenia-bosch">Yevhenia Bosh</a>. At first no head of government was agreed, and the chair went for the time being to the Secretary of Internal Affairs. State security was handled by the All-Russian Extraordinary Committee, and Bosh and Vladimir Antonov, commander-in-chief of the Soviet Ukrainian forces, took orders from Lenin.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Secretariat moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kyiv</a> in late January 1918 after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> captured the city. On 4 March 1918 Bosh resigned in protest at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-brest-litovsk">Treaty of Brest-Litovsk</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mykola-skrypnyk">Mykola Skrypnyk</a> was elected president, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volodymyr-zatonsky">Volodymyr Zatonsky</a> temporarily in charge of foreign affairs. As the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-powers">Central Powers</a> advanced, the Second All-Ukrainian Congress of Soviets in March declared Soviet Ukraine independent of Soviet Russia, and the government withdrew to Yekaterinoslav and in April to Taganrog.</p><p>On 4 April 1918 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-stalin">Joseph Stalin</a> telegraphed Zatonsky protesting against drawing the Don into the war with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and saying that it was time to stop playing at government and republic. Skrypnyk replied with a formal protest to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-peoples-commissars">Sovnarkom</a>, insisting that Taganrog was part of the Ukrainian People's Republic and that only its population could decide which Soviet federation to belong to. On 18 April 1918 the Secretariat was disbanded and reorganised with the Central Executive Committee into the Insurgency Bureau, or Povstanburo, whose members included Zatonsky, Skrypnyk, Andriy Bubnov and Heorhiy Pyatakov.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “People's Secretariat” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The government, called the <b>People's Secretariat</b>, included Yevhenia Bosh, Mykola Skrypnyk and Volodymyr Zatonsky.</blockquote><a href="/state/ukrainian-peoples-republic-of-soviets">Ukrainian People's Republic of Soviets</a></li></ul></section>
